  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <b>Implement DynamoDB Global Tables. Deploy an Auto Scaling Group behind an Elastic Load Balancer in each of the two regions hosting your application layer. Configure Route53 with latency-based DNS and failover across the ELBs in both regions.</b></p><p><b>DynamoDB Global Tables</b> are designed for active-active, multi-region replication, which means you can write and read to DynamoDB in multiple regions, providing the kind of resilience you need in case of total regional outages. With this set-up, if one region fails, your API can automatically fail over to the healthy region without data loss or a lengthy recovery process. Using Application Load Balancers and Auto Scaling Groups in both regions, combined with Route53's latency routing and failover, ensures your API is always available with minimal interruption.</p><p>The other options have the following issues:<br><i>Use a master-standby DynamoDB replication across regions, with a single standby in another region. For your application, provision Auto Scaling Groups with ELBs in both regions and configure Route53 latency DNS records pointing to the respective ELBs.</i>: DynamoDB does not natively support a 'master-standby' configuration for automatic failover. Setting it up this way leads to possible write conflicts, and the manual failover process would take longer than acceptable for your requirements.<br><i>Create a multi-region DynamoDB table and use a cross-region ELB targeting a cross-region Auto Scaling group, directing Route53 latency DNS records with failover to the cross-region ELB.</i>: There is no such feature as a 'multi-region table' or 'cross-region ELB' with AWS. Cross-region ELBs and Auto Scaling Groups do not exist, making this option invalid.<br><i>Set up master-standby DynamoDB cross-region replication with one standby in a separate region. Configure a cross-region ELB for a cross-region Auto Scaling Group, and use Route53 latency DNS failover aiming at the cross-region ELB.</i>: Similar to the previous point, this option incorrectly references AWS components that don't exist or are unsupported, and it also relies on manual failover mechanics, which violate your requirements.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> For true multi-region, highly available APIs backed by DynamoDB, always look for <b>DynamoDB Global Tables</b> and leverage AWS's native failover solutions to minimize downtime and recovery complexity.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your API depends on DynamoDB, and you must ensure it's highly available even in the event of a major AWS region outage. You can accept a few minutes of reduced performance during such an event, but expect the system to return to normal operation soon after. What is an effective solution to achieve this?</p>",
